        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Is the AJOOSOS 2-in-1 Under Desk Treadmill Any Good? The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>We could not confirm anything about the AJOOSOS 2-in-1 under desk treadmill beyond
            the title of its Amazon listing: AJOOSOS’s own store sells no treadmills, the listing would
            not open for us on 5 November 2026, and we found no independent review.</strong> If you buy
            one, buy it from a listing Amazon fulfils, at a price you would be comfortable with if the
            figures turn out optimistic, and check it against the list below before you pay.
          </p>
          <p>
            That is not an accusation. A great many walking pads on Amazon carry names that tell you
            nothing about who built them, and plenty of those pads are perfectly usable for slow
            walking at a desk. AJOOSOS is an unusual case only because the name belongs to an
            established scooter seller, which can make the treadmill feel better documented than it
            is. A scooter store’s reputation for scooters tells you nothing about a walking pad’s motor
            or deck, and on the day we looked, the store itself did not list the treadmill.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you would rather not take the risk, the cards further down include two 2-in-1 pads from
            brands with their own US stores, at $170 and $210, whose main figures we could check, plus a
            sturdier flat pad for heavier walkers. And if you have the AJOOSOS listing open in another
            tab, the <a href="#what-to-check" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">checklist below</a>
            tells you what to look for first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-we-found" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. What We Found When We Looked for AJOOSOS Treadmills</h2>
<p>
            Here is exactly what we did and what came back, so you can weigh it yourself.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>AJOOSOS’s own store.</strong> AJOOSOS runs a US web store at ajoosos.com. On
            5 November 2026 its full catalogue held 74 products: adult electric scooters such as the
            X48, X500, X700 and X750, e-bike conversion kits, folding electric wheelchairs, kids’
            go-karts and motorcycles, and scooter parts. There was no treadmill, walking pad or
            fitness product of any kind.</li>
            <li><strong>Amazon.</strong> Search engines index an Amazon listing, ASIN B0GLNJTVH4,
            titled “AJOOSOS Foldable Walking Pad Treadmill for Home with Incline &amp; Handle Bar, 2 in 1
            Portable Under Desk, 2.0 HP Low Noise Treadmill with Remote Control”. Amazon returned a
            server error each time we tried to read it, so we could not see the price, the seller, the
            specification table or the reviews.</li>
            <li><strong>eBay and other listings.</strong> Searches for AJOOSOS with “treadmill” also
            returned eBay listings and other Amazon pages carrying the name, describing folding
            treadmills and walking pads with motors from 2 to 3.5 HP, weight limits from 220 to 300 lb,
            inclines from 3 to 10% and even a <a href="/best-dog-treadmills-for-small-dogs/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>small dog treadmill</a>. eBay blocked us from reading them,
            so we cannot tell which are genuinely AJOOSOS-branded and which simply match the search.</li>
            <li><strong>Independent coverage.</strong> We found no review, no entry in the walking-pad
            trackers we use for other brands, no manual and no owner discussion we could tie to an
            AJOOSOS treadmill.</li>
            <li><strong>Safety records.</strong> We searched the CPSC recall database for AJOOSOS by
            recall title and by manufacturer and found no recall, for treadmills or anything else.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The most likely explanation is the ordinary one: a seller with an established Amazon brand
            registration has added walking pads, sourced from a factory that sells the same machine to
            other labels, to its range on the marketplace, without adding them to its own web store.
            That is common, and it is not in itself a warning sign. But it means there is no brand page
            to check the listing against, and that the AJOOSOS name may cover more than one machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            So the useful question is not whether AJOOSOS is a good brand. It is whether the specific
            listing in front of you gives you enough to buy safely, and whether it is cheaper than a pad
            whose figures someone other than the seller has checked.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-2-in-1-means" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. What “2-in-1 Under Desk Treadmill” Actually Means</h2>
<p>
            A 2-in-1 under desk treadmill is a walking pad with a handle bar that folds up and down. With
            the handle folded flat, it is a low, slim pad that slides under a standing desk, and the
            speed is usually capped at about 4 mph for walking. With the handle raised, the console in
            the handle unlocks a higher top speed, often 6 to 7.5 mph, so you can jog. That is the whole
            of the “2-in-1”: one machine, two modes, chosen by the position of the handle.
          </p>
          <p>
            It is a useful design, but the running mode needs reading with care. A 2-in-1 pad still has
            a short, narrow belt, typically 38 to 40 inches long and 15 to 16 inches wide, a small motor
            and a thin deck. That is fine for a brisk walk and tolerable for a short, gentle jog, but it
            is not a running treadmill, and the 6 or 7 mph top speed tells you what the motor controller
            allows, not what the machine is comfortable doing for half an hour. Our guide to
            <a href="/can-you-run-on-a-walking-pad/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">running on a walking pad</a>
            covers what is realistic.
          </p>
          <p>
            Some listings, including the AJOOSOS title, add an incline. On budget pads this is almost
            always a manual incline: you lift the front of the deck onto a fixed foot or riser, which
            gives one or two settings rather than a motorised range. That still makes walking harder,
            which is the point, but check whether the listing describes a fixed angle or a powered
            adjustment, and whether the stated percentage is the same everywhere it appears. Our guide
            to <a href="/best-walking-pads-with-incline/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pads with incline</a>
            explains the difference.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Does the handle make it safer?</h3>
          <p>
            Somewhat. A handle bar gives you something to steady yourself on while you find your
            footing, and it carries the stop button and display at hand height. But on most 2-in-1 pads
            it is a single short upright, not a pair of side rails, so it is not a substitute for the
            handrails on a full treadmill if balance is the reason you want one. Our roundup of
            <a href="/best-walking-pads-with-handrail/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pads with handrails</a>
            covers models with fuller support.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-to-check" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. What to Check on the AJOOSOS Listing Before You Buy</h2>
<p>
            Work through these in order. If the listing fails the first three, we would not buy from it
            at any price.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Who is actually selling and shipping it.</strong> Look at “Ships from” and
            “Sold by” in the buy box. A listing shipped by Amazon gives you Amazon’s own returns
            process; one shipped by a third-party seller means that seller handles the return. Open the
            seller’s profile for a business name and address.</li>
            <li><strong>The return window and who pays return shipping.</strong> A 2-in-1 pad weighs
            40 to 60 lb. If returns are not free, sending one back can cost a large share of what you
            paid. Read the terms on the listing itself.</li>
            <li><strong>The warranty, in writing.</strong> Look for a stated length and who provides
            it. Because AJOOSOS’s web store does not list the treadmill, ask the seller through Amazon
            who handles warranty claims and how parts are supplied, and keep the answer.</li>
            <li><strong>A model number.</strong> A model number lets you match the listing to a manual,
            to identical pads sold under other names and to any safety notice. Look in the
            specification table and on the box photos.</li>
            <li><strong>Consistency within the listing.</strong> Check that the motor, weight limit,
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt size</a>,
            speeds and incline are the same in the title, the bullet points, the images and the
            specification table. The title we could see gives 2.0 HP; if the table says 2.5 or 3.0, the
            figures have not been checked by anyone.</li>
            <li><strong>Both speed ranges.</strong> A 2-in-1 listing should give one range with the
            handle down and one with it up. If it gives only the top speed, assume the walking mode is
            capped at about 4 mph.</li>
            <li><strong>Reviews that describe your use.</strong> Sort by most recent and read the one-
            and three-star reviews for mentions of the <a href="/treadmill-belt-slipping/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>belt slipping</a>, the motor cutting out, the remote
            failing or the handle working loose. Make sure the photos in reviews show the same machine
            as the listing, since listings are sometimes reused for different products.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Our guide to <a href="/buying-a-treadmill-on-amazon/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">buying a treadmill on Amazon</a>
            explains the seller and returns side in more detail, and our
            <a href="/treadmill-return-policies/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill return policies</a>
            page compares what each retailer offers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="reading-the-specs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Reading the Figures on a Budget 2-in-1 Pad</h2>
<p>
            Even when you can read a listing in full, these are the four figures most likely to mislead
            on a budget 2-in-1 pad.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Horsepower.</strong> Figures of 2.0, 2.5 or 3.0 HP on a walking pad are almost
            always peak ratings, the short burst a motor can produce. Independent trackers that read
            motor labels regularly find continuous output under 1 HP on pads advertised at 2.5 HP. At
            walking pace that is usually enough, and a 2.0 HP badge is not necessarily weaker than a
            3.0 HP one on another listing. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill horsepower guide</a>
            explains the difference.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Weight limit.</strong> This is the figure that most often differs between a listing,
            its manual and the maker’s own pages; one of the checked pads on this page carries three
            different capacities across its maker’s listings. Leave a margin of at least 50 lb between
            your weight and the stated limit, and assume the lowest figure you see. Our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-weight-capacity-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill weight capacity</a>
            explains why.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Belt size.</strong> Check the belt, not the overall length. Most 2-in-1 pads have a
            belt of 38 to 40 inches by 15 to 16 inches, which suits walking but feels short for a jog or
            for a tall walker with a long stride.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Noise.</strong> “Below 45 dB” appears on almost every walking-pad listing. Where
            reviewers have measured, they have usually recorded figures in the 50s and 60s at walking
            speed. Expect it to be audible on a video call, and put it on a mat if you live upstairs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Checked 2-in-1 Pads and Alternatives at a Similar Price</h2>
<p>
            If what drew you to the AJOOSOS listing was a handle, an incline and a low price, these
            cost between $170 and $300 on 5 November 2026, come from brands that sell through their own
            US stores, and have main figures we could check. Where sources disagree, the figure is left
            off the card and the description says so. None is perfect; each card says what is wrong
            with it.
          </p>
          <p>
            For the cheapest checked 2-in-1, the UREVO Strol 2E. For a handle and an incline, the UMAY
            U20F, though its weight limit is unclear. And for a heavier walker who does not need a
            jogging mode, the MERACH W50 TrekPad. Our
